steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 4
      - mark 1 B 7
      - mark 1 C 12
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]Distros &ndash; The Portability Substrate}

      Prior tours have focused upon _portable_ **EMS** pro&shy;grams &ndash; from {[cu]BlinkerP} {[tr,101/01/02]}&thinsp; to {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} {[tr,102/06/03]}.

      By default, these programs execute on a **Raspberry Pi Pico** simulator; in principle, they can run unchanged elsewhere.

      <div class="em-info">

      You'll soon use the clickable <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span> {[em]Board} icon in the **Status Bar** to select alternative target platforms.

      </div>

      {[cu]BoardC}{[bm,A]} &ndash; found in a logical {[cs]'@$distro'} bucket &ndash; plays a pivotal role in ensuring portability.

      Besides exporting delegates like {[cn]AppLed}{[bm,B]} , {[cu]BoardC} binds proxies like {[cu]Common}.{[cn]BusyWait}{[bm,C]} to distro-specific code.

      But {[cu]BoardC} remains "the tip of an iceberg" &ndash; the lat&shy;ter lik&shy;ened to a *complete* **EMS** distro introduced by this tour.

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,account_tree]EMS Distro Architecture}

      Whenever building a program, the **EMS** translator requires a _target context_ {[dc,1]} which selects:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} a {[em]Board}, which identifies a target hardware platform<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} a {[em]Setup}, which defines target program build options

      {[em]Board} in turn binds the logical name {[cs]'@$distro'}{[dc,2]} to a physical folder inside an **EMS** distro package [`rpi.2040`].

      The {[cs]'@$distro/BoardC.em'} composite largely connects platform-specific target modules with portable clients {[dc,3]}.

      The {[cs]'@$distro/BuildC.em'} composite then orchestrates generation of build artifacts {[dc,4]} for the target program.

      <div class="em-info">

      The distro-centric {[cu]BuildC} composite (fundamental to **EMS** translation) will merit its own dedicated tour.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,snippet_folder]Inside a Distro Package}

      Each **EMS** distro package contains one required bucket {[dc,D]}, named after the containing package in an obvious manner.

      <div class="em-info">

      The logical bucket name {[cs]'@$distro'} used in **EMS** code in fact resolves to physical bucket {[dc,D]}.

      </div>

      Though not mandated, each distro package will typically feature an `mcu` bucket {[dc,M]} with target-specific content.

      <div class="em-info">

      A quick peek at an earlier tour stop {[tr,101/04/04]} shows how our {[cu]BoardC} composite works with units from {[dc,M]}.

      </div>

      Some **EMS** distro packages will contain additional buckets following the same canonical naming pattern:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} `*.gist.*`<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} `*.radio.*`<br>

  - cmds:
      - view 4
    text: |
      {[ht,settings]Selecting a Board + Setup}

      {[in]DRAFT}

      * board identifies target hardware
      * board implies a distro package
      * point to the board status item
      * callback to earlier BoardC exposure

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,$build]From Sources to a Build}

      {[in]DRAFT}

      * BoardC binds portable services to target implementations
      * BuildC drives generated build output
      * name both bridges without opening either source
      * keep generated artifacts at map level

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]Extra Credit}

      {[in]DRAFT}

      * select another board
      * select another setup
      * rebuild a familiar portable program
      * observe status and output changes
      * no hardware or execution required

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]Next Steps}

      {[in]DRAFT}

      * Tour 1 showed the normal steady-state architecture
      * Tour 2 introduces bare-metal bring-up
      * minimal BuildC
      * Gist01
      * direct $R access
      * generated artifacts and Wokwi execution
